# Normalize all text files to LF in the repo, AND check them out as LF.
#
# The `eol=lf` half is load-bearing and is NOT the default. Without it git
# checks a text file out using core.eol precisely when core.autocrlf is
# false -- the setting on this machine -- and core.eol is unset, so it
# defaults to `native`, i.e. CRLF on Windows. What that produced was a
# working tree whose line endings flipped per file on no schedule: git
# rewrote a file to CRLF every time it touched one (checkout, switch,
# stash, and pre-commit's own stash/restore around each commit), while the
# editors and agent tools that wrote the same files in between left LF. On
# 2026-09-09 that was 361 files CRLF against 8 LF, the 8 being simply the
# ones most recently edited by hand.
#
# None of which shows up HERE, because `text=auto` normalizes on the way
# into the index either way -- so the tree looks clean while being
# arbitrarily mixed. It showed up DOWNSTREAM. scripts/copy-copilot.ps1
# vendors this payload into client repos as real committed files, so every
# flip landed there as the entire payload modified with nothing visibly
# changed (159 files, 2026-09-09). That script normalizes on its own now,
# but the instability was here, so it is fixed here too.
#
# In .gitattributes rather than core.eol deliberately: this is committed,
# so it holds for every clone on every machine, where local config is
# invisible state a fresh clone silently loses.
#
# Files already committed with CRLF are left alone until an explicit
# `git add --renormalize .`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts: pin CRLF in the working tree. cmd.exe mis-parses
# LF-only batch files (goto/label resolution), and CRLF is the safe default
# for scripts that legacy PowerShell hosts or double-click execution touch.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell scripts: pin LF. A bash script that picks up CRLF fails with
# cryptic "\r: command not found" errors.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Extensionless bash scripts, pinned BY NAME because the *.sh rule above
# cannot see them. Both are run directly, so a CRLF checkout breaks them the
# same way.
#
# This pin USED TO BE load-bearing, for the reason the `*` rule above now
# spells out at length: a bare `text=auto` left checkout following core.eol,
# which is `native` here, so an unpinned text file came back CRLF and these
# two broke. Adding `eol=lf` to that rule covers them, so the pin is
# belt-and-braces now rather than the only thing holding them.
#
# It stays anyway. The `*` rule is one line that governs everything and is
# exactly the kind of line someone edits for an unrelated reason; these two
# scripts execute directly and have no extension to fall back on, so they
# keep a pin that names them. (Load-bearing until 2026-09-09, verified
# 2026-09-01.)
#
# There is no glob for "file with a shebang": a new extensionless script needs
# its own line here.
scripts/instructions-log text eol=lf
scripts/bootstrap-pre-commit text eol=lf

# Binary assets. `* text=auto` already leaves these alone in practice --
# git sniffs the NUL bytes in a PNG header and skips normalization -- but
# the detection is heuristic, so say it outright rather than relying on
# the sniff. `binary` is the shorthand for `-text -diff`: no EOL
# conversion in either direction, and no attempt to render a textual diff.
*.png binary
